#
# Security tier: default
#
# What a plain build ships, and on by default: build.sh composes it into
# every image built without profiles on its line. Every tool and builtin is
# built, and each behaves 1:1 with bash, dash and GNU for anything a script
# does. STRICT=1 is the only lean: a divergence only where benign input is
# unaffected and hostile input was dangerous (escaping a remote peer's utmp
# host field, refusing a symlinked component under mkdir -p).
#
# The tiers, each a whole answer -- name one last, after the rest of the
# default line, in place of this one:
#
#   sh build.sh debug_none limbo desktop wifi serial sec_hardened
#
#   sec_reference   the reference exactly, holes and all (STRICT=0)
#   sec_default     this
#   sec_workstation the default's userspace, and the kernel hardened in every
#                   region that costs a program nothing it expects (not
#                   lockdown, not the 32-bit ABI): a machine somebody sits at
#   sec_hardened    refuse where default sanitises (STRICT=2), and the kernel
#                   at its hardened level in every region
#   sec_locked      a kiosk or appliance: a short allow list of tools,
#                   STRICT=3, and the kernel locked in every region
#
# The kernel is the same story in several sizes. Every tier stands on
# sec_baseline, which is what a mainstream kernel ships on and what this
# tree's allnoconfig base did not have until it was written down (stack
# protector, usercopy and FORTIFY checks, slab freelist hardening, the CPU
# vulnerability mitigations, W^X on riscv64, a floor under mmap). The
# hardened and locked tiers add the regions' own levels on top.
#
# A region is a part of the hardening that can move alone. Name its profile
# after the tier to take that one further, or take a whole tier and keep a
# region at a gentler level by composing that region's profile before it
# (the last word on an option wins, and a region's file says so with
# `#> overrides` where it undoes another's):
#
#   kernel     sec_mem_hardened   sec_mem_locked       heap, stack, zeroing
#              sec_cpu_hardened   sec_cpu_locked       speculation, IBT, SMT
#              sec_surface_...    sec_surface_locked   what a process can ask
#              sec_dma_hardened   sec_dma_locked       IOMMU, hotplug buses
#              sec_lockdown_...   sec_lockdown_locked  integrity, confidentiality
#   userspace  sec_files_...      sec_text_...         paths, terminals
#              sec_net_...        sec_host_...         the wire, media and sysctls
#              sec_bowl_...       sec_exec_hardened    bowls, what may start
#
# (each ..._hardened beside its ..._locked; sec_exec has no locked level.)
#
# Only the default promises the reference's behaviour. The others are a
# choice, and what they refuse is refused on purpose.
#

#
# Yama. Floodlight's restricted launches keep a confined child from being
# attached to by its siblings, which needs ptrace_scope 1 or above -- the
# default Yama boots with. The LSM list the kernel defaults to already
# names yama. INTEGRITY is on by default under SECURITY and brings in
# nothing any profile asks for, so it stays out.
#
#> includes sec_baseline

CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y
CONFIG_INTEGRITY=n

CONFIG_MOONWATER_STRICT=1
